1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:09,407
In 1666, a single spark in a bakery destroyed 13,000 homes, 87 churches, and most of London.

2
00:00:09,357 --> 00:00:12,509
But the real story isn't about fire.

3
00:00:12,509 --> 00:00:15,077
It's about human failure.

4
00:00:15,077 --> 00:00:19,696
London in 1666 was a city waiting to burn.

5
00:00:19,696 --> 00:00:24,547
Timber-framed buildings pressed shoulder to shoulder along medieval streets.

6
00:00:24,547 --> 00:00:27,876
The summer had been scorching and bone-dry.

7
00:00:27,876 --> 00:00:31,069
The wood was seasoned to perfection for burning.

8
00:00:31,069 --> 00:00:35,226
Thomas Farriner's bakery on Pudding Lane was the epicenter.

9
00:00:35,226 --> 00:00:39,547
His oven was allegedly left unattended on Saturday night.

10
00:00:39,547 --> 00:00:44,859
By dawn on Sunday, September 2nd, flames had already taken hold.

11
00:00:44,859 --> 00:00:49,465
But here's what most people don't know: the fire could have been stopped.

12
00:00:49,465 --> 00:00:54,588
The Lord Mayor of London, Sir Thomas Bloodworth, was woken with the news.

13
00:00:54,588 --> 00:00:56,585
His response?

14
00:00:56,585 --> 00:00:59,044
A woman could piss it out.

15
00:00:59,044 --> 00:01:03,759
He refused to demolish surrounding buildings to create firebreaks.

16
00:01:03,759 --> 00:01:07,753
By the time he changed his mind, it was too late.

17
00:01:07,753 --> 00:01:10,824
The fire had grown beyond control.

18
00:01:10,824 --> 00:01:17,386
Samuel Pepys, a diarist and naval administrator, watched the destruction unfold.

19
00:01:17,386 --> 00:01:20,702
His account is one of the most vivid we have.

20
00:01:20,702 --> 00:01:26,979
I saw a fire as one entire arch of fire above a mile long: it made me weep to see it.

21
00:01:26,979 --> 00:01:35,172
The churches, houses are all on fire and flaming at once, and a horrid noise the flames made and the cracking of the houses.

22
00:01:35,172 --> 00:01:42,332
Pepys made a decision that sounds bizarre today: he buried his Parmesan cheese and wine in his garden.

23
00:01:42,332 --> 00:01:47,414
But in 1666, this was practical crisis management.

24
00:01:47,414 --> 00:01:52,522
Hard cheese was imported, expensive, and remarkably durable.

25
00:01:52,522 --> 00:01:59,370
With London descending into chaos, Pepys was safeguarding a compact store of wealth and calories.

26
00:01:59,370 --> 00:02:02,373
The fire raged for four days.

27
00:02:02,373 --> 00:02:10,851
It consumed everything in its path: 13,200 houses, 87 churches, most of the City of London.

28
00:02:10,851 --> 00:02:14,248
Only six deaths were officially recorded.

29
00:02:14,248 --> 00:02:18,188
But historians believe the true number was much higher.

30
00:02:18,188 --> 00:02:23,297
The poor, the homeless, the undocumented—they simply vanished.

31
00:02:23,297 --> 00:02:26,408
The fire reached the Thames and stopped there.

32
00:02:26,408 --> 00:02:30,457
Water on one side, open ground on the other.

33
00:02:30,457 --> 00:02:33,895
The city was saved from complete annihilation.

34
00:02:33,895 --> 00:02:40,049
In the aftermath, three plans were presented to King Charles II for rebuilding the city.

35
00:02:40,049 --> 00:02:44,778
Christopher Wren, John Evelyn, and Robert Hooke each had a vision.

36
00:02:44,778 --> 00:02:47,984
None of their plans were fully adopted.

37
00:02:47,984 --> 00:02:50,946
The old street lines were retained.

38
00:02:50,946 --> 00:02:58,134
But Wren's masterpiece survived: St. Paul's Cathedral, rebuilt from the ashes, still stands today.

39
00:02:58,134 --> 00:03:08,025
The fire had swept away the medieval city—with all its inefficiency, its fire hazard, its disease-prone density—and created space for something new.

40
00:03:08,025 --> 00:03:13,677
The London that emerged was more orderly, more regulated, more ambitious.

41
00:03:13,677 --> 00:03:16,395
The fire didn't destroy London.

42
00:03:16,395 --> 00:03:18,487
It transformed it.

43
00:03:18,487 --> 00:03:24,098
When you walk through London today, you're walking through a city that rose from ashes.

44
00:03:24,098 --> 00:03:26,734
Every brick tells that story.

45
00:03:26,734 --> 00:03:30,389
The Great Fire of London wasn't just a disaster.

46
00:03:30,389 --> 00:03:33,799
It was the moment a city learned to rebuild.

